\DOC satisfy_prover

\TYPE {satisfy_prover : prover}

\SYNOPSIS
Prover for existential goals that chooses witnesses from the context facts.

\DESCRIBE
This is a prover (see {mk_prover}) included in the standard simpsets
{basic_ss} and {empty_ss}, mainly so that the simplifier can instantiate
variables in the conditions of conditional rewrites. Its state is a list of
`facts', obtained from the context theorems it is augmented with by splitting
up conjunctions and stripping universal quantifiers, discarding implications.
Applied to a goal {?x1 ... xn. p1 /\ ... /\ pk}, it tries to choose witnesses
{t1,...,tn} so that each instantiated conjunct
{pi[t1,...,tn/x1,...,xn]} is one of the facts (up to alpha-conversion),
backtracking over the possible choices. The free variables of the facts are
treated as constants, so this is a matter of matching each conjunct, with the
variables {x1,...,xn} instantiable, against the facts.

Unlike the usual convention for provers, the theorem returned is not the
existential goal itself but the instance with the witnesses plugged in, i.e.
{|- p1[t1,...,tn/x1,...,xn] /\ ... /\ pk[t1,...,tn/x1,...,xn]}, so that the
witnesses can be read off by matching (this is what {find_witnesses} does).
The simplifier's prover-use function {basic_prover} accepts such an instance
as a proof of the existential.

\FAILURE
The value itself is not a function, but applying it with {apply_prover} fails
unless the term is an existential that can be satisfied as described above.

\EXAMPLE
{
  # let pr = augment satisfy_prover
      [ASSUME `(x:A) IN s`; ASSUME `(x:A) IN t`; ASSUME `FINITE(t:A->bool)`];;
  val pr : prover = Prover (<fun>, <fun>)

  # apply_prover pr `?u. (x:A) IN u /\ FINITE u`;;
  val it : thm = FINITE t, x IN t |- x IN t /\ FINITE t
}

\COMMENTS
The idea and name come from Don Syme's {SATISFY} in hol98, still found in
HOL4. Since the facts are only matched, not unified, universally quantified
facts such as {|- !s. s SUBSET UNIV} are only useful when the variable names
happen to coincide.

\SEEALSO
apply_prover, augment, basic_prover, basic_ss, empty_ss, find_witnesses,
mk_prover, SIMP_CONV.

\ENDDOC
